  "footnotes": [
    "Workload: hack/memory-cake on branch memory-cake-harness. A scripted agent (no LLM) drives ONE `dagger mcp` session in a vito/dang checkout (pinned 150ddb6, its dagger.lock; workspace modules go, editor, committer, context, plus the cake's own Dang and Go tool modules). Each turn: 3 file reads; edits to a Go package that grows a function and a test (File.withReplaced, as the editor's edit); go build ./... and go vet; go test of 3 packages under otelgotest, so every test lands as a span; tool calls whose state grows every turn (a Dang module's notes, the context module's blockWrite, a Go-SDK module's items); an edit to a Dang module that is then loaded again (asModule) and evaluated in place (DangEval at it). Every 3rd turn: FindSpans (by test name, failed, by path), ReadLogs and ReadTrace on a test span, FindCalls, InspectCall, ListServices.",
    "Both bars are at turn 290. BEFORE was stopped there at a memory cap (Sys passed 14 GiB: 15.2 GB, HeapInuse 7.2 GB, after 60 min), not at its time limit, to keep the shared host from running out of memory overnight; AFTER then ran exactly 290 turns (51 min) and ended at HeapInuse 5.6 GB, Sys 11.4 GB.",
    "Layers are pprof inuse_space after a forced GC (/debug/pprof/heap?gc=1), each sample in exactly one layer: the first of hack/memory-cake/layers' ordered stack-frame rules matching any frame of its stack (spans, logs, other telemetry, content-hash, leases, prepared, schema, arbitrary, dagql), else other. Growth per turn is a least-squares slope over the second half of each run's checkpoints (every 10 turns).",
    "Schema building did not shrink here (451 -\u003e 491 MiB): the schema PRs dedupe the same module sets rebuilt across client roots and sessions, while this single agent loads a NEW module every turn (the edited probe module) in one session, which no memo can share. The 19.7 GB live engine of the estimate had many concurrent workers repeating the same module sets; this workload does not reproduce that, so this cake says nothing about those PRs' effect there.",
    "Other and dagql result bookkeeping (about 3.3 GiB of each bar, growing about 16 MiB per turn on both sides) likely hold mostly the workload's own growing state (not verified stack by stack): every call on a tool whose state grows is a new result holding the whole state, so they grow quadratically in turns. No PR addresses them.",
    "Not covered: LoadTrace of a Cloud trace (no Cloud credentials in this sandbox), so #15218's release of idle imported traces is not measured; #15219 lowers FindCalls' transient peak, which a post-GC profile does not see.",
    "`dagger mcp` serves Workspace-taking tools the host checkout instead of the conversation's bound workspace, so edits go through DangEval's `ws` rather than the editor's write/edit. The editor's grep (every 4th turn) failed on both sides with a host search error, 72 failed calls per side; no other call failed.",
    "One run per side: no repetition, so differences of a few percent in a layer are within noise."
  ]
